WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.910
Every test, I turn marks into grades by

2
00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:07.850
hand: seventy and above is an A, fifty

3
00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:10.850
to sixty-nine a B, and so on.

4
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.300
Last term I gave a pupil with

5
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.500
exactly fifty the wrong grade.

6
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.340
A program never forgets the rule.

7
00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.830
Today: decisions in Python,

8
00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.370
with if, elif and else.

9
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.870
A decision starts with a comparison.

10
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.020
Is the mark at least fifty?

11
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.400
In Python that’s mark, greater

12
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.140
than or equal, fifty.

13
00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:38.950
Two equals signs ask: are these the same?

14
00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:42.790
One equals sign stores a value, so

15
00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.290
mixing them up is the most common bug.

16
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:49.410
An if line ends with a colon, and the

17
00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.610
lines under it are indented by four

18
00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:51.970
spaces.

19
00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.600
They run only when the condition is true.

20
00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.070
Else catches every other case.

21
00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.860
The indent tells Python which

22
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.760
lines belong to which choice.

23
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.500
For more than two choices, use

24
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.800
elif, short for else if.

25
00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.220
Python checks from the top and stops at

26
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.920
the first condition that’s true.

27
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.460
So put the highest band first: seventy,

28
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.440
then fifty, then everything else.

29
00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.350
It’s like sorting exam papers into piles.

30
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.720
Is it seventy or more?

31
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.060
First pile.

32
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.040
If not, is it fifty or more?

33
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:32.360
Second pile.

34
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.660
Everything else goes on the last pile.

35
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.080
Each paper lands on exactly one pile.

36
00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:40.910
Quick check!

37
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.340
With that program, a pupil

38
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.420
scores exactly fifty.

39
00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.350
Which grade does she get?

40
00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.440
Grade B.

41
00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:53.810
Greater than or equal

42
00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:55.490
includes fifty itself.

43
00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:59.070
Fifty is where the grade changes, so

44
00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.770
it’s exactly the mark to test.

45
00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.020
Always test the boundaries: forty-nine,

46
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.940
fifty, sixty-nine and seventy.

47
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.560
Put it together with input, and

48
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.240
you have a small tool.

49
00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.440
It asks for a mark, turns it into a

50
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.100
number, and prints the grade.

51
00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.300
Type forty marks, and every

52
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.960
grade follows the same rule.

53
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:26.000
Expect three bugs when pupils start.

54
00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.070
A missing colon at the end of the if line.

55
00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:32.350
One equals sign where two are needed.

56
00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.920
And a line that isn’t indented, so Python

57
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.860
doesn’t know it belongs to the if.

58
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.240
In the app, open Bug hunt.

59
00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.960
Bug one is a missing colon and bug

60
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.180
two is a single equals sign.

61
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:51.080
Run each one, read the error and its

62
00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.800
line, fix it and run it again.

63
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:55.760
Your turn.

64
00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:59.180
A pupil writes: if mark equals

65
00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.160
fifty, with a single equals sign.

66
00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:03.430
What happens?

67
00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:05.890
A syntax error.

68
00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.560
Remember: two equals signs

69
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.040
compare; one stores.

70
00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.840
An if line ends with a colon,

71
00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:15.920
and its lines are indented.

72
00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:18.990
elif checks the next band, and the

73
00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.470
first true condition wins.

74
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:22.310
Test the boundaries.

75
00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.750
Try it in class tomorrow: Human if.

76
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.440
Write a grading rule on the board.

77
00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.750
Pupils hold up made-up marks, and the

78
00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:35.730
class decides each grade by following the

79
00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.510
rule line by line, starting at the top.

80
00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:42.130
Try forty-nine, fifty and seventy.
